"""More homeowner guides (added 2026-11-18). Plain, sourced or general statements only; no invented prices or stats."""

# Each: (path, title tag, h1, meta description, intro, body html, crumb, photo key, faq list, guide-list blurb, related paths)
G2 = [
("/metal-roof-vs-shingles/", "Metal Roof vs Shingles: Which Is Better? | Bedrock Roof", "Metal roof vs. asphalt shingles: which is better?",
 "Metal roofing vs asphalt shingles compared: lifespan, up-front cost, hail and wind, noise, energy use and resale, plus who each one suits.",
 "The two most common choices for a new roof on a U.S. home, compared side by side.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Asphalt shingles cost less up front and are what most U.S. homes have. Metal roofing usually costs more to install but lasts much longer, often 40 years or more versus roughly 15 to 30 for shingles, and it handles wind and wildfire embers well. If you plan to stay in the home a long time, metal can cost less over the roof's life. If you're selling in a few years or working to a tight budget, architectural shingles are usually the better value.</div>
<h2>Side-by-side</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Asphalt shingles</th><th>Metal (standing seam or panels)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Up-front cost</td><td>Lowest of the common options</td><td>Higher, often much higher for standing seam</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical lifespan</td><td>About 15&ndash;30 years</td><td>About 40&ndash;70 years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind</td><td>Good with high-wind rated shingles and proper nailing</td><td>Very good when installed to the panel's rating</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hail</td><td>Standard shingles bruise; Class 4 versions resist better</td><td>Resists punctures but can dent; dents may be cosmetic only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fire</td><td>Most carry a Class A fire rating</td><td>Noncombustible; Class A assemblies common</td></tr>
<tr><td>Noise in rain</td><td>Quiet</td><td>Usually quiet over solid decking and underlayment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repairs</td><td>Easy and cheap to patch</td><td>Needs a crew familiar with metal</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>When shingles make more sense</h2><ul><li>You expect to sell within about 10 years.</li><li>Your budget is fixed and the roof needs replacing now.</li><li>Your HOA limits roof materials or colors.</li></ul>
<h2>When metal makes more sense</h2><ul><li>You plan to stay 20+ years and want one roof for the long haul.</li><li>You're in wildfire country or a high-wind coastal area.</li><li>You get heavy snow and want it to shed easily (add snow guards over doors and walkways).</li></ul>
<h2>Hail and insurance</h2><p>In hail country, ask your insurer how they treat cosmetic dents on metal. Some policies exclude cosmetic damage to metal roofs, which can matter after a big storm. Class 4 impact-resistant shingles are the other option many hail-belt homeowners compare. See <a href="/impact-resistant-shingles/">Class 4 shingles</a>.</p>
<h2>Get both priced</h2><p>Prices vary a lot by region and roof shape, so ask a roofer to quote both a shingle and a metal option on the same visit. Compare the full scope: tear-off, decking repair, underlayment, flashing, ventilation and warranty.</p>""",
 "Metal vs shingles", "metal",
 [("Is a metal roof louder than shingles?", "Usually not by much. Over solid roof decking with underlayment, most homeowners say rain on a metal roof isn't noticeably louder. Metal over open framing, like on a barn, is louder."),
  ("Does a metal roof attract lightning?", "No. Metal doesn't make a strike more likely. If lightning does hit, metal is noncombustible and spreads the energy."),
  ("Can metal go over existing shingles?", "Sometimes, if local code allows it and the deck is sound. Many roofers still recommend a full tear-off so they can inspect and repair the decking."),
  ("Which adds more resale value?", "A newer roof of either kind helps a sale. Metal can be a selling point in some markets, but buyers rarely pay back the full extra cost.")],
 "Cost, lifespan, hail, wind and who each suits.",
 ["/roofing-materials/", "/how-long-does-a-roof-last/", "/impact-resistant-shingles/", "/roof-replacement-cost/"]),

("/roof-inspection/", "What Happens During a Roof Inspection? | Bedrock Roof", "What happens during a roof inspection?",
 "What a roofer checks during a roof inspection, how long it takes, what to ask, and what you should get in writing afterward.",
 "Know what a good inspection covers so you can tell a thorough roofer from a rushed sales pitch.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> A roof inspection usually takes 30 to 90 minutes for a typical house. A roofer checks the shingles or panels, flashing, vents, gutters, attic and signs of leaks, takes photos, and then tells you whether the roof needs a repair, a replacement or nothing yet. Ask for the photos and a written summary either way.</div>
<h2>What gets checked</h2><ul><li><b>Roof covering:</b> missing, cracked, curling or bruised shingles; granule loss; loose or dented metal panels.</li><li><b>Flashing:</b> around chimneys, skylights, walls and valleys, where most leaks start.</li><li><b>Penetrations:</b> pipe boots, vents and exhaust fans.</li><li><b>Gutters and drainage:</b> clogs, sagging and granules collecting in gutters.</li><li><b>Attic (if accessible):</b> daylight through boards, water stains, mold, ventilation and insulation.</li><li><b>Decking:</b> soft or spongy spots that suggest rot.</li></ul>
<h2>What you should get afterward</h2><ol><li>Photos of any damage, ideally with something for scale.</li><li>A plain explanation: repair, replace, or monitor.</li><li>A written quote that lists materials, scope, permit, cleanup and warranty if work is recommended.</li></ol>
<h2>Questions to ask</h2><ul><li>Can you show me the damage in the photos?</li><li>Is this storm damage, age, or an installation problem?</li><li>What happens if I wait a year?</li><li>Is a repair realistic, or would you replace?</li></ul>
<h2>Red flags</h2><p>Be careful if someone damages the roof during the inspection, won't show you photos, or pushes you to sign a contract or insurance paperwork before you've had time to think. See <a href="/roofing-scams/">roofing scams</a>.</p>
<h2>When to get one</h2><p>After a major hail or wind storm, before buying a home, when your roof is 15+ years old, or any time you see a leak or stain. Commercial roofs should be checked twice a year; see our <a href="/commercial-roof-maintenance/">commercial checklist</a>.</p>""",
 "Roof inspection", "crew2",
 [("Is a roof inspection free?", "Many roofers offer free inspections when you're considering a repair or replacement. A home-sale inspection or a certified report for insurance may cost a fee."),
  ("How long does a roof inspection take?", "Often 30 to 90 minutes for a typical house. Larger or steeper roofs and attic checks take longer."),
  ("Do I need to be home?", "It helps, so the roofer can check the attic and show you photos, but many will inspect the outside without you there."),
  ("Can a drone inspect my roof?", "Yes, some roofers use drones for steep or fragile roofs. A drone can't check the attic or feel for soft decking, so it's not always a full substitute.")],
 "What a roofer checks and what to get in writing.",
 ["/signs-you-need-a-new-roof/", "/hail-damage-roof/", "/choosing-a-roofer/", "/roofing-scams/"]),

("/roof-replacement-permit/", "Do You Need a Permit to Replace a Roof? | Bedrock Roof", "Do you need a permit to replace your roof?",
 "Whether a roof replacement or repair needs a building permit, who pulls it, what inspections happen, and what can go wrong if you skip it.",
 "In most of the U.S., a full roof replacement needs a permit. Here's how it works and why it matters.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Most cities and counties require a building permit for a full roof replacement, and many also require one for larger repairs. Small repairs like replacing a few shingles often don't need one. Rules are set locally, so check with your city or county building department. A licensed roofer usually pulls the permit and schedules the inspection for you.</div>
<h2>Why the permit matters</h2><ul><li>An inspector checks the work against the building code, including underlayment, nailing, flashing and ventilation.</li><li>Unpermitted work can cause trouble when you sell or when you file an insurance claim.</li><li>Some insurers and lenders ask for proof of a permitted, inspected roof.</li><li>In hurricane-prone areas, a permitted roof may help you qualify for wind-mitigation discounts.</li></ul>
<h2>Who pulls it</h2><p>Usually the roofing contractor. In many places only a licensed contractor (or the owner of a home they live in) can pull one. Be wary if a roofer asks you to pull the permit in your own name; that can make you responsible for code issues. Put "contractor obtains all permits" in the contract.</p>
<h2>What it costs</h2><p>Permit fees vary widely by city and are often based on the job's value. Ask for the fee to be listed on your quote.</p>
<h2>Inspections</h2><p>Some places inspect only at the end. Others also inspect mid-job, for example after the decking or underlayment goes on, especially in high-wind zones. Don't make final payment until the roof passes.</p>
<h2>HOAs</h2><p>If you have an HOA, you may also need its approval for material and color, separate from the city permit.</p>""",
 "Roof permits", "tearoff",
 [("Do I need a permit to repair my roof?", "Small repairs often don't need one, but larger repairs or replacing a big section might. Your local building department sets the threshold."),
  ("Can I get in trouble for an unpermitted roof?", "You may be asked to get a permit after the fact, open up the work for inspection, or fix code issues, and it can complicate a home sale or insurance claim."),
  ("How do I check if my roofer pulled a permit?", "Many cities have online permit search by address. You can also ask the roofer for the permit number and call the building department."),
  ("Does a roof-over (second layer) need a permit?", "Usually yes, and some places don't allow more than two layers or ban overlays entirely.")],
 "When you need one, who pulls it, and why it matters.",
 ["/choosing-a-roofer/", "/roof-replacement-cost/", "/roofing-scams/", "/roof-replacement-process/"]),

("/roof-warranty/", "Roof Warranties Explained: Manufacturer vs Workmanship | Bedrock Roof", "Roof warranties explained",
 "The difference between a shingle manufacturer warranty and a roofer's workmanship warranty, what voids them, and what to ask before you sign.",
 "Two different warranties cover a new roof. Here's what each one does and how to protect both.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> A new roof usually has two warranties. The <b>manufacturer warranty</b> covers defects in the shingles or other materials. The <b>workmanship warranty</b> comes from your roofer and covers installation mistakes, which cause most early roof problems. Get the workmanship warranty in writing, ask how many years it lasts, and keep your invoice and product details.</div>
<h2>Manufacturer warranty</h2><p>Covers defective materials. Many shingle warranties are prorated after an initial period, so coverage shrinks over time. Some manufacturers offer enhanced warranties, which can include some labor coverage, when a roof is installed by a credentialed contractor using a full system of their components. Read what's actually covered; "lifetime" often has conditions.</p>
<h2>Workmanship warranty</h2><p>Covers installation errors such as poor flashing, wrong nailing or bad ventilation. Length varies a lot between roofers. It's only worth something if the company is still in business, so ask how long they've operated locally.</p>
<h2>What can void a warranty</h2><ul><li>Installing shingles in a way the manufacturer doesn't allow.</li><li>Poor attic ventilation.</li><li>Later work by another contractor on the same area.</li><li>Not registering the warranty when registration is required.</li></ul>
<h2>Questions to ask before signing</h2><ol><li>How long is your workmanship warranty, and is it in the contract?</li><li>Which manufacturer warranty will this roof get, and will you register it?</li><li>Is the warranty transferable if I sell?</li><li>What do I need to do to keep it valid?</li></ol>""",
 "Roof warranties", "shingles",
 [("What does a lifetime roof warranty mean?", "Usually that the manufacturer covers material defects for as long as you own the home, often prorated after the first years. It rarely covers labor or storm damage unless stated."),
  ("Does a roof warranty cover storm damage?", "Generally no. Storm damage is usually a homeowner's insurance matter. Some shingles have separate wind ratings or limited wind warranties."),
  ("Is a roof warranty transferable?", "Many manufacturer warranties can transfer once, sometimes with a notice or fee. Check the terms, as this can help when you sell."),
  ("Who do I call with a warranty problem?", "Start with your roofer for leaks or installation issues. For a suspected material defect, the manufacturer will usually want photos, the product details and proof of purchase.")],
 "Manufacturer vs workmanship coverage and what voids it.",
 ["/choosing-a-roofer/", "/roofing-materials/", "/how-long-does-a-roof-last/", "/roof-replacement-process/"]),

("/best-time-to-replace-roof/", "Best Time of Year to Replace a Roof | Bedrock Roof", "When is the best time of year to replace a roof?",
 "The best season to replace a roof by climate, how weather affects shingles and scheduling, and why waiting for spring can backfire.",
 "Weather, demand and your region all affect when a roof replacement goes smoothest.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Late spring through early fall is the most common roofing season in most of the U.S., with fall often the sweet spot: mild temperatures help shingles seal, and storms are less frequent in many areas. Roofers can install year-round in warm southern climates. If your roof is leaking or storm-damaged, don't wait for the "best" season; a temporary fix or replacement now usually costs less than water damage later.</div>
<h2>By season</h2><ul><li><b>Spring:</b> good weather in many places, but rain can cause delays and spring hail can create a backlog.</li><li><b>Summer:</b> long days and dry weather in much of the country; very high heat can make shingles easier to scuff and is hard on crews.</li><li><b>Fall:</b> often ideal. Moderate temperatures help self-sealing shingle strips bond before winter.</li><li><b>Winter:</b> possible in mild climates. In cold regions, crews may limit work, and shingles may need hand-sealing.</li></ul>
<h2>By region</h2><ul><li><b>Gulf and Atlantic coasts:</b> try to finish before peak hurricane season (August to October).</li><li><b>Hail belt (Texas to the Dakotas, Colorado):</b> demand spikes after spring and summer hail; booking early helps.</li><li><b>Northern states:</b> fall is popular because of winter; book early.</li><li><b>Desert Southwest:</b> spring and fall avoid the hottest months.</li></ul>
<h2>Scheduling tips</h2><ol><li>Get quotes a month or two before you want the work.</li><li>Ask about the roofer's backlog and weather-delay policy.</li><li>Don't sign with a door-knocker just because a storm just passed. See <a href="/roofing-scams/">roofing scams</a>.</li></ol>""",
 "Best time to replace", "crew3",
 [("Can a roof be replaced in winter?", "Yes in many places. Roofers watch the temperature and may hand-seal shingles in cold weather. In snowy regions, winter jobs are less common."),
  ("Is it cheaper to replace a roof in the off-season?", "Sometimes. Some roofers have more scheduling flexibility in slower months, but material prices don't drop much by season."),
  ("How far ahead should I book a roofer?", "Several weeks is common, and longer after a big regional storm. Getting quotes early gives you time to compare."),
  ("Should I wait to replace a leaking roof?", "No. Get the leak stopped now, even with a temporary repair, to prevent damage inside the house.")],
 "Season by season and region by region.",
 ["/signs-you-need-a-new-roof/", "/roof-replacement-process/", "/roof-replacement-cost/", "/storm-damage/"]),

("/roof-replacement-process/", "Roof Replacement Process: What to Expect | Bedrock Roof", "What to expect during a roof replacement",
 "Step by step: what happens on roof replacement day, how long it takes, how to prepare your home, and the final walkthrough.",
 "From the dumpster arriving to the final walkthrough, here's how a typical roof replacement goes.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Most roof replacements on a typical single-family home take one to three days once work starts. The crew tears off the old roof, repairs damaged decking, installs underlayment, flashing and the new roof covering, then cleans up with magnets for nails. Larger, steeper or more complex roofs, and metal, tile or slate, can take longer.</div>
<h2>Before the job</h2><ul><li>Sign a written contract with the scope, materials, price, payment schedule, permit and warranty.</li><li>Confirm the permit and the start date.</li><li>Move cars, patio furniture and grills away from the house.</li><li>Take down wall hangings on top-floor walls; hammering can shake them.</li><li>Cover items in the attic to catch dust.</li><li>Plan for pets and kids; it's loud.</li></ul>
<h2>Day by day</h2><ol><li><b>Setup:</b> dumpster or trailer, tarps over plants and siding.</li><li><b>Tear-off:</b> old shingles and underlayment come off down to the decking.</li><li><b>Decking:</b> rotted or soft boards are replaced. Contracts often price this per sheet, so ask in advance.</li><li><b>Underlayment and ice/water shield</b> in valleys, eaves and around penetrations, as code requires.</li><li><b>Flashing, drip edge and vents.</b></li><li><b>New roof covering.</b></li><li><b>Ridge cap and final details.</b></li><li><b>Cleanup:</b> magnetic sweep for nails, debris hauled away.</li></ol>
<h2>Final walkthrough</h2><p>Walk the property with the crew lead. Ask for photos of the decking and underlayment stages, check gutters and the yard for nails, confirm the permit passed inspection, and get warranty paperwork before final payment. See <a href="/roof-warranty/">roof warranties</a>.</p>""",
 "Replacement process", "crew1",
 [("How long does a roof replacement take?", "One to three days of work is typical for an average single-family home with asphalt shingles. Weather, roof size, steepness and material can extend that."),
  ("Do I need to be home during a roof replacement?", "Not usually, but be reachable in case the crew finds rotten decking or needs a decision."),
  ("What if they find rotten wood?", "The roofer replaces damaged decking, usually at a price per sheet agreed in the contract. Ask to see photos."),
  ("Will my gutters be removed?", "Usually not, though some may be loosened to install drip edge. Ask whether gutter work is included.")],
 "Prep, day-by-day steps and the final walkthrough.",
 ["/roof-replacement-permit/", "/roof-warranty/", "/choosing-a-roofer/", "/roof-replacement-cost/"]),
("/roofing-estimate/", "How to Read and Compare Roofing Estimates | Bedrock Roof", "How to read and compare roofing estimates",
 "What a good roofing estimate should include, line by line, how to compare quotes fairly, and the red flags that mean you should keep looking.",
 "Two quotes for the same roof can look very different. Here's how to compare them fairly.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> A good roofing estimate is written and itemized. It names the shingle or panel brand and product line, says whether the old roof is torn off, lists underlayment, ice and water shield, flashing, drip edge and ventilation, explains how rotten decking is priced, and includes the permit, cleanup, warranty and payment schedule. Compare quotes on the same scope, not just the bottom line.</div>
<h2>What should be on the estimate</h2><ul><li><b>Company details:</b> legal name, address, phone, and license number where your state licenses roofers.</li><li><b>Roof size</b> in squares (1 square = 100 sq ft) and the waste factor.</li><li><b>Tear-off:</b> how many layers are removed, and disposal.</li><li><b>Decking:</b> price per sheet for replacing rotten wood.</li><li><b>Underlayment</b> type, and <b>ice and water shield</b> locations.</li><li><b>Flashing:</b> new or reused at chimneys, walls and skylights.</li><li><b>Ventilation:</b> ridge vents, intake vents, any changes.</li><li><b>Roof covering:</b> brand, product line and color.</li><li><b>Permit, cleanup, warranty</b> and <b>payment schedule</b>.</li></ul>
<h2>How to compare quotes fairly</h2><ol><li>Put the quotes side by side and check each line above.</li><li>Ask the lower bidder what's missing, and the higher bidder what's extra.</li><li>Check that the same product line is quoted. "Architectural shingles" can mean very different products.</li><li>Compare workmanship warranty length, not just the manufacturer's.</li></ol>
<h2>Red flags</h2><ul><li>A one-line quote ("new roof: $X") with no detail.</li><li>A large cash deposit before materials arrive.</li><li>Pressure to sign today or "this price expires tonight."</li><li>An offer to cover or "waive" your insurance deductible, which is illegal in some states.</li></ul>
<p>More: <a href="/roof-replacement-cost/">roof replacement cost</a> and <a href="/roofing-scams/">roofing scams</a>.</p>""",
 "Roofing estimates", "tearoff",
 [("How many roofing quotes should I get?", "For a full replacement, two or three written quotes is a common rule of thumb. For a small repair, one trusted roofer may be enough."),
  ("Why are roofing quotes so different?", "Different products, scope (tear-off, decking, flashing, ventilation), labor costs, overhead and warranty length. Line up the scope first."),
  ("What is a roofing square?", "A square is 100 square feet of roof surface. A roof is measured in squares, plus extra for waste on cuts, hips and valleys."),
  ("How much deposit is normal for a roof?", "It varies, and some states cap deposits on home improvement work. Be cautious about paying a large share before materials are delivered.")],
 "What a good quote includes and how to compare.",
 ["/roof-replacement-cost/", "/choosing-a-roofer/", "/roofing-scams/", "/roof-warranty/"]),

("/ice-dams/", "Ice Dams on Your Roof: Causes, Damage and Prevention | Bedrock Roof", "Ice dams: what causes them and how to prevent them",
 "Why ice dams form on roofs, the damage they cause, safe ways to deal with one now, and the long-term fixes: insulation, ventilation and ice and water shield.",
 "If you see a thick ridge of ice and icicles at the edge of your roof, here's what's happening and what to do.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Ice dams form when heat escaping from the house melts snow on the upper roof, and the meltwater refreezes at the colder roof edge. Water then backs up under the shingles and can leak into ceilings and walls. The long-term fix is to keep the roof deck cold with better attic air sealing, insulation and ventilation, plus ice and water shield along the eaves when the roof is replaced.</div>
<h2>Signs of an ice dam</h2><ul><li>A thick ridge of ice along the eaves and heavy icicles.</li><li>Water stains or dripping on ceilings or exterior walls during a thaw.</li><li>Ice forming inside the soffits or gutters.</li></ul>
<h2>What to do now (safely)</h2><ul><li>Don't climb on an icy roof or chip at ice with tools. It's dangerous and damages shingles.</li><li>A roof rake used from the ground can pull snow off the lower few feet of the roof.</li><li>Professional ice dam removal with low-pressure steam is safer for the roof than hammers or chisels.</li><li>Catch drips inside and photograph any damage for insurance.</li></ul>
<h2>Long-term fixes</h2><ol><li><b>Air-seal the attic floor</b>: gaps around light fixtures, pipes and the attic hatch let warm air in.</li><li><b>Add insulation</b> to recommended levels for your climate.</li><li><b>Balance ventilation</b> with intake at the soffits and exhaust at the ridge.</li><li><b>Ice and water shield</b> at the eaves when you replace the roof; many cold-climate building codes require it.</li></ol>
<p>Heat cables can help in trouble spots but don't fix the cause. More: <a href="/roof-leak/">roof leaking now?</a></p>""",
 "Ice dams", "roofsky",
 [("Does homeowners insurance cover ice dam damage?", "Many policies cover sudden water damage inside the home from an ice dam, but often not the ice removal itself or long-term maintenance problems. Check your policy and call your agent."),
  ("Do gutters cause ice dams?", "Not by themselves. Clogged gutters can make ice buildup worse, but the main cause is heat loss melting snow higher on the roof."),
  ("Do heated roof cables work?", "They can keep channels open in problem areas, but they use electricity and don't fix the heat loss behind the problem."),
  ("Will a new roof stop ice dams?", "A new roof with ice and water shield at the eaves limits leaks, but ice dams can still form unless attic insulation and ventilation are fixed.")],
 "Causes, safe removal and long-term fixes.",
 ["/roof-leak/", "/roof-ventilation/", "/signs-you-need-a-new-roof/", "/roof-insurance-claim/"]),

("/roof-ventilation/", "Roof and Attic Ventilation Explained | Bedrock Roof", "Roof and attic ventilation explained",
 "Why attic ventilation matters, how intake and exhaust vents work together, signs of poor ventilation and what to ask your roofer during a replacement.",
 "Ventilation is easy to overlook, but it affects shingle life, ice dams, moisture and comfort.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Most roofs over an attic need balanced ventilation: cool air comes in low at the soffits (intake) and warm, moist air leaves high at the ridge (exhaust). Poor ventilation can trap heat and moisture, shorten shingle life, cause mold and contribute to ice dams. Shingle manufacturers commonly require proper ventilation for full warranty coverage, so ask your roofer to check it during any replacement.</div>
<h2>Intake and exhaust</h2><ul><li><b>Intake:</b> soffit vents or edge vents near the eaves.</li><li><b>Exhaust:</b> ridge vents, box (static) vents, or powered fans near the top.</li><li>Mixing different exhaust types on the same attic can short-circuit airflow; roofers usually pick one.</li></ul>
<h2>Signs of poor ventilation</h2><ul><li>Very hot attic in summer, high cooling bills.</li><li>Frost or condensation on the underside of the roof deck in winter.</li><li>Mold, musty smell or rusty nails in the attic.</li><li>Ice dams in winter.</li><li>Shingles aging faster on one section of roof.</li></ul>
<h2>What to ask during a replacement</h2><ol><li>Is my current ventilation adequate for the attic size?</li><li>Are the soffit vents blocked by insulation?</li><li>What exhaust will you install, and will it meet the shingle maker's requirements?</li></ol>
<p>Note: some homes use an unvented ("conditioned") attic with insulation under the roof deck. That's a different design, so don't add vents without professional advice.</p>""",
 "Roof ventilation", "home3",
 [("How much attic ventilation do I need?", "A common rule in building codes is 1 square foot of net free vent area per 150 square feet of attic floor, or 1 per 300 in some cases with balanced intake and exhaust. Your roofer or local code will confirm."),
  ("Are ridge vents better than box vents?", "Ridge vents spread exhaust evenly along the peak and are common on new roofs. Box vents work too. The key is balance with intake at the eaves."),
  ("Can too much ventilation be a problem?", "Mixing exhaust types or having too little intake can pull air the wrong way, including weather. Balance matters more than quantity."),
  ("Does ventilation affect my shingle warranty?", "Often yes. Many shingle manufacturers require adequate ventilation for full warranty coverage.")],
 "Intake, exhaust and signs of trouble.",
 ["/ice-dams/", "/how-long-does-a-roof-last/", "/roof-warranty/", "/roof-inspection/"]),

("/roof-financing/", "Roof Financing Options for Homeowners | Bedrock Roof", "How to pay for a new roof: financing options",
 "Ways homeowners pay for a roof replacement: savings, insurance, contractor financing, personal loans, home equity, and local assistance programs, with pros and cons.",
 "A new roof is a big expense. Here are the common ways homeowners pay for one, and what to watch out for.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Homeowners usually pay for a new roof with savings, an insurance claim (if the damage is from a covered event like hail or wind), contractor-arranged financing, a personal loan, or a home equity loan or line of credit. Compare the total cost of borrowing, not just the monthly payment, and be careful with "0% interest" offers that charge deferred interest if not paid off in time.</div>
<h2>Common options</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Good for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Insurance claim</td><td>Sudden storm or hail damage</td><td>Deductible; age or wear isn't covered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contractor financing</td><td>Convenience; promo rates</td><td>Deferred interest; fees; high rates after promo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal loan</td><td>No home collateral</td><td>Higher rates for weaker credit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Home equity loan / HELOC</td><td>Lower rates for homeowners with equity</td><td>Your home secures the loan; closing costs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Credit card</td><td>Small repairs</td><td>High interest if not paid quickly</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Assistance programs</h2><p>Some homeowners qualify for help. Low-income and older homeowners can ask their local housing agency, Area Agency on Aging, or USDA Rural Development (for rural homes) about repair grants or loans. Some states also offer grants to strengthen roofs against hurricanes.</p>
<h2>Tips</h2><ol><li>Get your quotes first, then compare financing separately.</li><li>Read the fine print on any promotional rate.</li><li>Never sign financing paperwork you don't understand on the spot.</li></ol>
<p>More: <a href="/insurance-roof-replacement/">does insurance cover roof replacement?</a></p>""",
 "Roof financing", "couple",
 [("Can I finance a new roof with bad credit?", "Possibly, through some contractor programs or lenders that work with lower credit scores, but expect higher rates. Compare total cost carefully."),
  ("Is a home equity loan a good way to pay for a roof?", "It often has lower rates than unsecured loans, but your home is collateral. It suits homeowners with equity and steady income."),
  ("Are there grants for roof replacement?", "Some local, state and federal programs help eligible homeowners, often based on income, age, disability or rural location. Availability varies by area."),
  ("Does insurance pay for an old roof?", "Insurance generally pays for sudden damage from covered events, not wear and age. Older roofs may be paid at actual cash value.")],
 "Insurance, loans, contractor financing and grants.",
 ["/roof-replacement-cost/", "/insurance-roof-replacement/", "/roofing-estimate/", "/roof-repair-or-replace/"]),
("/architectural-vs-3-tab-shingles/", "Architectural vs 3-Tab Shingles: Differences | Bedrock Roof", "Architectural vs. 3-tab shingles: what's the difference?",
 "Architectural (dimensional) shingles vs 3-tab shingles: look, thickness, wind rating, lifespan, cost and why most new roofs now use architectural.",
 "The two main types of asphalt shingles, and why most roofers now quote architectural.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> 3-tab shingles are thin, flat, single-layer shingles with a uniform look. Architectural (also called dimensional or laminated) shingles are made of two or more bonded layers, so they're thicker, have a textured look, usually carry higher wind ratings and longer warranties, and cost somewhat more. Most new residential roofs in the U.S. now use architectural shingles, and some manufacturers have reduced or discontinued 3-tab lines.</div>
<h2>Side-by-side</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>3-tab</th><th>Architectural</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Construction</td><td>Single layer</td><td>Two or more laminated layers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Look</td><td>Flat, uniform rows</td><td>Dimensional, shadowed, can mimic wood shake or slate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight</td><td>Lighter</td><td>Heavier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind rating</td><td>Often lower</td><td>Often higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical life</td><td>Shorter</td><td>Longer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Price</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Somewhat higher</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>When 3-tab still makes sense</h2><ul><li>Repairing an existing 3-tab roof so patches match.</li><li>Sheds, garages or rentals where the lowest price matters most.</li></ul>
<h2>Beyond architectural</h2><p>"Designer" or luxury shingles are heavier still and mimic slate or shake. Class 4 impact-resistant versions are available in many architectural lines. See <a href="/impact-resistant-shingles/">Class 4 shingles</a> and <a href="/roofing-materials/">all roofing materials</a>.</p>""",
 "Shingle types", "shingles",
 [("Are architectural shingles worth the extra cost?", "For most homeowners, yes: better wind resistance, a longer warranty and better curb appeal for a modest price difference on a full roof."),
  ("Can you mix 3-tab and architectural shingles?", "On separate roof sections, sometimes, but they look different side by side. For repairs, roofers try to match the existing type."),
  ("How can I tell which shingles I have?", "3-tab shingles show evenly spaced cutouts and a flat look. Architectural shingles look layered and uneven, with no regular tabs."),
  ("Do architectural shingles need special installation?", "They follow the manufacturer's nailing pattern like any shingle; the key is a crew that installs to spec so the warranty applies.")],
 "Look, wind rating, lifespan and cost.",
 ["/roofing-materials/", "/metal-roof-vs-shingles/", "/impact-resistant-shingles/", "/how-long-does-a-roof-last/"]),

("/wind-mitigation-inspection/", "Wind Mitigation Inspection: Lower Your Insurance | Bedrock Roof", "Wind mitigation inspections: how they can lower your insurance",
 "What a wind mitigation inspection is, what the inspector checks (roof deck, roof-to-wall connections, shape, openings), and how it can reduce home insurance premiums.",
 "Common in Florida and other hurricane-prone areas, a wind mitigation inspection documents the features that help your home resist wind.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> A wind mitigation inspection documents how well your home's roof and openings resist hurricane winds. In Florida, insurers are required by law to offer discounts for qualifying wind-resistant features, and the inspection is usually recorded on the state's standard form (OIR-B1-1802). A new roof installed to current code, with the right nailing, secondary water barrier and roof-to-wall connections, can improve your results.</div>
<h2>What the inspector checks</h2><ul><li><b>Roof covering</b> and whether it meets current building code, with permit date.</li><li><b>Roof deck attachment</b>: nail size and spacing holding the decking to the rafters.</li><li><b>Roof-to-wall attachment</b>: toe nails, clips, single or double wraps (hurricane straps).</li><li><b>Roof shape</b>: hip roofs generally score better than gable roofs.</li><li><b>Secondary water resistance</b>: a sealed roof deck under the covering.</li><li><b>Opening protection</b>: impact windows, doors and shutters.</li></ul>
<h2>Who does it</h2><p>Licensed home inspectors, contractors, engineers and other professionals allowed by your state. Fees vary; ask before booking.</p>
<h2>Timing with a new roof</h2><p>If you're replacing your roof, ask the roofer about upgrades that affect the report, such as sealing the deck and re-nailing the decking to current code. Then get a new inspection after the permit passes and send it to your insurer.</p>
<p>More: <a href="/roofers/florida/">Florida roofers and licensing</a> and <a href="/storm-damage/">after storm damage</a>.</p>""",
 "Wind mitigation", "home2",
 [("How much can a wind mitigation inspection save?", "It depends on your insurer, home and features found. Some homeowners see meaningful savings on the windstorm portion of their premium; others see little change."),
  ("How long is a wind mitigation report valid?", "In Florida, insurers generally accept the report for up to five years, as long as nothing material changes. Check with your insurer."),
  ("Do I need a wind mitigation inspection outside Florida?", "Some insurers in other coastal states offer similar discounts, such as for roofs built to FORTIFIED standards. Ask your agent."),
  ("Does a new roof automatically lower my premium?", "Not automatically. Send your insurer the permit and, where applicable, an updated wind mitigation report.")],
 "What's checked and how it can cut premiums.",
 ["/storm-damage/", "/roof-insurance-claim/", "/roof-replacement-permit/", "/old-roof-insurance/"]),

("/replace-roof-before-solar/", "Should You Replace Your Roof Before Installing Solar? | Bedrock Roof", "Should you replace your roof before going solar?",
 "Why the age and condition of your roof matter before installing solar panels, the cost of removing and reinstalling panels later, and how to coordinate the two jobs.",
 "Solar panels can last 25 years or more. If your roof won't, plan ahead.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> If your roof is more than about halfway through its expected life, or a roofer finds problems, it's usually smarter to replace it before installing solar. Solar panels commonly last 25 years or more, and removing and reinstalling them later for a roof replacement adds cost and hassle. Get a roof inspection first, and coordinate the roofer and solar installer so penetrations and flashing are done right.</div>
<h2>Why it matters</h2><ul><li>Panels cover much of the roof, making later repairs harder.</li><li>Detach-and-reset of panels for a reroof is a separate, often significant expense.</li><li>Some solar warranties cover roof penetrations only if the roof was in good shape.</li></ul>
<h2>How to decide</h2><ol><li>Find your roof's age and material. See <a href="/how-long-does-a-roof-last/">how long roofs last</a>.</li><li>Get a <a href="/roof-inspection/">roof inspection</a> and a written opinion on remaining life.</li><li>If replacement is likely within the next several years, do it first.</li></ol>
<h2>Coordinating the two jobs</h2><ul><li>Ask whether the solar installer or roofer will flash the mounts, and who warranties leaks.</li><li>Consider durable materials under the array, like architectural shingles or metal.</li><li>Keep records of both warranties together.</li></ul>""",
 "Roof before solar", "roofsky",
 [("How old is too old for a roof before solar?", "There's no single cutoff, but many installers want a roof with at least as much remaining life as the panel warranty or plan for replacement first. An inspection gives a better answer than age alone."),
  ("Does installing solar damage the roof?", "Properly installed mounts are flashed and sealed. Leaks usually trace back to poor installation, so choose an installer with a workmanship warranty."),
  ("Can I bundle roof replacement into solar financing?", "Some solar companies and lenders allow it. Compare the total cost and terms with separate financing."),
  ("Is metal roofing good for solar?", "Yes. Standing seam metal allows clamp-on mounts without penetrations and outlasts most panels.")],
 "Timing, costs and coordinating both jobs.",
 ["/how-long-does-a-roof-last/", "/roof-inspection/", "/metal-roof-vs-shingles/", "/roof-financing/"]),

("/old-roof-insurance/", "Old Roof and Home Insurance: What to Know | Bedrock Roof", "Old roof and home insurance: what to know",
 "How roof age affects homeowners insurance: higher premiums, actual cash value payouts, non-renewal notices, roof inspections, and what you can do.",
 "Insurers pay close attention to roof age. Here's how it can affect your policy and your options.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> As a roof ages, many insurers charge more, switch roof coverage from replacement cost to actual cash value (which deducts for depreciation), require an inspection, or decline to renew the policy. Rules vary by insurer and state. If you get a notice about your roof, ask exactly what the insurer needs, get an independent inspection, and compare other insurers before replacing a roof that still has useful life.</div>
<h2>What insurers may do</h2><ul><li><b>Raise premiums</b> or deductibles for older roofs.</li><li><b>Pay actual cash value (ACV)</b> for the roof instead of replacement cost (RCV). See <a href="/roof-insurance-claim/">ACV vs RCV</a>.</li><li><b>Require a roof inspection</b> or a roof certification.</li><li><b>Non-renew</b> the policy. Many states require advance written notice.</li></ul>
<h2>What you can do</h2><ol><li>Read the notice carefully and ask what would satisfy the insurer.</li><li>Get a <a href="/roof-inspection/">roof inspection</a> with photos and an estimate of remaining life.</li><li>Ask an independent agent to shop other carriers.</li><li>If you replace the roof, send the permit and invoice to your insurer, and ask about discounts.</li><li>Contact your state insurance department if you think a notice doesn't follow state rules.</li></ol>""",
 "Old roof and insurance", "home5",
 [("Can my insurance company drop me because of my roof's age?", "In many states, insurers can decline to renew based on roof age or condition, usually with advance notice. Some states have added limits. Check your state insurance department's rules."),
  ("What age roof do insurers consider old?", "It varies by insurer and material. Asphalt shingle roofs past roughly 15 to 20 years often get extra scrutiny."),
  ("Will insurance pay for a new roof if mine is old?", "Only if a covered event, like hail or wind, caused damage. Wear and age aren't covered, and older roofs may be paid at actual cash value."),
  ("Does a roof certification help?", "It can. A certification or inspection report showing remaining life may satisfy some insurers or buyers.")],
 "Premiums, ACV payouts, non-renewal and options.",
 ["/insurance-roof-replacement/", "/roof-insurance-claim/", "/roof-inspection/", "/signs-you-need-a-new-roof/"]),

("/emergency-roof-tarp/", "Emergency Roof Tarping: What to Do After Damage | Bedrock Roof", "Emergency roof tarping after storm damage",
 "How to protect your home with an emergency roof tarp after a storm, when to call a pro, what insurance usually covers, and how long a tarp lasts.",
 "After a storm opens up your roof, a tarp buys time and limits water damage until repairs.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> If storm damage lets water in, an emergency tarp can prevent much bigger interior damage. Most homeowner policies expect you to take reasonable steps to prevent further damage, and many cover the cost of emergency tarping as part of a covered claim. Climbing a damaged, wet roof is dangerous, so hire a roofer or emergency service when you can. Keep photos and receipts.</div>
<h2>Right after the storm</h2><ol><li>Stay safe. Stay off the roof in wind, rain or darkness, and watch for downed power lines.</li><li>Photograph damage outside and inside before anything is moved.</li><li>Catch water inside with buckets and move belongings.</li><li>Call a roofer or tarping service, and call your insurer to start a claim.</li></ol>
<h2>How a proper tarp is installed</h2><ul><li>A heavy-duty tarp extending well past the damaged area, ideally over the ridge so water runs off.</li><li>Fastened with wood battens screwed or nailed through the tarp's edges, not just weighed down with bricks.</li><li>Edges sealed so wind can't get under it.</li></ul>
<h2>How long a tarp lasts</h2><p>A tarp is temporary. Sun and wind wear it down, so schedule permanent repairs as soon as possible. After hurricanes, some areas have free federal or local temporary roofing programs; check official announcements.</p>
<p>More: <a href="/storm-damage/">storm damage guide</a> and <a href="/roof-insurance-claim/">filing a claim</a>.</p>""",
 "Emergency tarp", "crew3",
 [("Does insurance pay for an emergency roof tarp?", "Often, as part of a covered claim, because policies expect you to prevent further damage. Keep the invoice and photos and check your policy."),
  ("Can I tarp my roof myself?", "It's possible on a low, dry, safe roof, but falls are a real risk. A roofer or tarping service is safer, especially on steep or wet roofs."),
  ("How long can a roof tarp stay on?", "It's meant to be temporary. Tarps degrade in sun and wind, so plan permanent repairs promptly."),
  ("Should I sign with the company that tarps my roof?", "You can hire a company to tarp without signing a full repair contract. Read anything carefully before signing, especially assignment-of-benefits forms.")],
 "Stay safe, limit damage and what insurance covers.",
 ["/storm-damage/", "/roof-leak/", "/roof-insurance-claim/", "/roofing-scams/"]),
("/roof-overlay-vs-tear-off/", "Roof Overlay vs Tear-Off: Can You Shingle Over? | Bedrock Roof", "Roof overlay vs. tear-off: can you put new shingles over old?",
 "Should you put a new roof over the old one or tear off to the deck? Code limits, cost trade-offs, warranty and insurance effects, and when an overlay is a bad idea.",
 "An overlay (also called a layover or re-roof) saves tear-off work, but it isn't always allowed or wise.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> An overlay puts new shingles over one existing layer, which skips tear-off labor and disposal. It's only an option when the old roof is flat, dry and sound, and when local code allows it. Most U.S. codes, based on the International Residential Code, don't allow a new layer over two existing layers. A full tear-off costs more but lets the roofer inspect and repair the decking, and it's what most manufacturers and roofers prefer.</div>
<h2>Overlay vs. tear-off</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Overlay</th><th>Tear-off</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Up-front cost</td><td>Lower (no removal or dump fees)</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deck inspection</td><td>Hidden problems stay hidden</td><td>Rot and soft spots found and fixed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Underlayment, flashing, ice barrier</td><td>Often left as-is</td><td>Replaced with new</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight</td><td>Adds a second layer of weight</td><td>One layer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lifespan</td><td>Often shorter; old bumps telegraph through</td><td>Full lifespan of the new material</td></tr>
<tr><td>Next replacement</td><td>Two layers must come off</td><td>One layer</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>When an overlay is a bad idea</h2><ul><li>The roof already has two layers.</li><li>The decking is soft, rotted or water-soaked, or the roof leaks in several places.</li><li>The old shingles are badly curled or buckled, so the new ones won't lie flat.</li><li>You live where ice dams form and need new ice-and-water barrier at the eaves.</li><li>You're filing an insurance claim. Many claims pay for removal of the damaged roof, so an overlay leaves money and inspection on the table.</li></ul>
<h2>Warranty and resale</h2><p>Some shingle manufacturers limit or exclude warranty coverage for overlays, or offer their best warranties only on tear-offs. Read the specific warranty before you agree. Home buyers and inspectors may also ask how many layers a roof has. See <a href="/roof-warranty/">roof warranties</a>.</p>
<h2>What to ask your roofer</h2><ul><li>How many layers are on the roof now?</li><li>Does our city or county allow an overlay here?</li><li>What does the shingle warranty say about overlays?</li><li>Can you quote both options so I can compare?</li></ul>""",
 "Overlay vs tear-off", "tearoff",
 [("How many layers of shingles are allowed?", "Most U.S. codes based on the International Residential Code allow at most two layers, so you can't add a new layer over two existing ones. Your local building department has the final say."),
  ("Is an overlay cheaper?", "Usually, because there's no tear-off labor or disposal. The savings can disappear if hidden deck damage causes problems later."),
  ("Can metal roofing go over shingles?", "Sometimes, with the right underlayment or furring strips and if code allows it. Many roofers still recommend a tear-off so the deck can be checked."),
  ("Does an overlay void my warranty?", "It depends on the manufacturer. Some reduce or exclude coverage for overlays. Read the warranty before you decide.")],
 "Code limits, cost trade-offs and warranty effects.",
 ["/roof-replacement-process/", "/roof-replacement-cost/", "/roof-warranty/", "/roof-repair-or-replace/"]),

("/roof-algae-black-streaks/", "Black Streaks on Your Roof: Algae, Moss and Lichen | Bedrock Roof", "Black streaks on your roof: algae, moss and lichen",
 "What causes black streaks and green moss on a roof, whether they cause damage, how to clean them safely without pressure washing, and how to prevent them.",
 "Dark stains are common on shingle roofs in humid areas. Here's what they are and what to do.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Dark streaks on asphalt shingles are usually a type of algae, often called Gloeocapsa magma. It's mostly cosmetic. Moss and lichen are different: they hold moisture and can lift shingles, so they're worth removing. Clean gently with a roof-safe cleaner and low pressure, never a pressure washer. Algae-resistant shingles and zinc or copper strips near the ridge help prevent regrowth.</div>
<h2>What you're looking at</h2><ul><li><b>Black or dark streaks</b> running down the roof: algae. It grows on the shady, damp sides of the roof first.</li><li><b>Green, fuzzy clumps:</b> moss. It traps water and can push shingle edges up.</li><li><b>Crusty, flat patches</b> that cling tightly: lichen. Removing it by force can tear granules off.</li></ul>
<h2>Does it damage the roof?</h2><p>Algae is mostly a looks problem, although dark streaks can make a roof absorb a bit more heat. Moss and heavy lichen are a real concern because they hold moisture against the shingles and under their edges, which can shorten the roof's life and lead to leaks.</p>
<h2>How to clean it safely</h2><ol><li>Hire a pro if the roof is steep, high or slippery. Wet, moss-covered roofs are dangerous.</li><li>Use a cleaner made for roofs and follow the label. Shingle makers and roofing groups often suggest a diluted bleach solution or a commercial roof cleaner applied at low pressure.</li><li>Rinse gently from the top down. Protect plants and keep runoff away from ponds and drains.</li><li>Don't pressure wash or scrape. It strips granules, which protect shingles from the sun and can void warranties.</li><li>Let dead moss loosen and fall off over time instead of prying it.</li></ol>
<h2>Prevention</h2><ul><li>Trim branches so the roof gets more sun and dries faster.</li><li>Keep gutters and valleys clear of leaves.</li><li>Zinc or copper strips near the ridge release metal ions in the rain that discourage growth.</li><li>When you replace the roof, choose algae-resistant (AR) shingles, which contain copper granules.</li></ul>""",
 "Roof algae and moss", "shingles",
 [("Are black streaks on my roof mold?", "They're usually algae, not mold. It's mostly cosmetic but can be cleaned and prevented."),
  ("Should I pressure wash my roof?", "No. Pressure washing strips the protective granules from shingles and can void the warranty. Use a roof-safe cleaner at low pressure."),
  ("Does homeowners insurance cover roof moss?", "Generally no. Moss and algae are treated as maintenance, not sudden damage."),
  ("Do I need a new roof because of moss?", "Usually not, if it's caught early. Heavy moss that has lifted or rotted shingles may need repairs.")],
 "What causes streaks and moss, safe cleaning and prevention.",
 ["/signs-you-need-a-new-roof/", "/how-long-does-a-roof-last/", "/roof-inspection/", "/roofing-materials/"]),

("/roof-flashing/", "Roof Flashing: What It Is and Why It Leaks | Bedrock Roof", "Roof flashing: what it is and why it leaks",
 "Roof flashing explained: step, counter, valley, drip edge and pipe boots, the signs of failing flashing, and whether to repair it or replace it with a new roof.",
 "Many roof leaks start not in the shingles but at the flashing where the roof meets something else.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Flashing is thin metal (or sometimes rubber) that seals the joints where a roof meets a wall, chimney, skylight, vent pipe or valley. Those joints are where water most often gets in. Rusted, loose or poorly sealed flashing is one of the most common causes of leaks, and it can often be repaired without replacing the whole roof.</div>
<h2>Types of flashing</h2><ul><li><b>Step flashing:</b> L-shaped pieces woven in with each course of shingles where a roof meets a side wall.</li><li><b>Counter flashing:</b> set into a chimney or wall and lapped over the step flashing.</li><li><b>Valley flashing:</b> lines the valley where two roof slopes meet.</li><li><b>Drip edge:</b> metal along the eaves and rakes that guides water into the gutter and protects the deck edge.</li><li><b>Pipe boots:</b> collars around plumbing vents. Rubber boots crack in the sun over time.</li><li><b>Skylight and chimney kits:</b> pre-formed flashing for those openings.</li></ul>
<h2>Signs of failing flashing</h2><ul><li>Stains on the ceiling or wall near a chimney, skylight or where a roof meets a wall.</li><li>Rust, gaps, or flashing that has pulled away.</li><li>Caulk or roofing cement smeared over flashing as a quick fix, which often fails again.</li><li>Cracked rubber around vent pipes.</li></ul>
<h2>Repair or replace?</h2><p>If the roof is in good shape, a roofer can usually replace the failing flashing or pipe boot on its own. If the roof is near the end of its life, it often makes sense to redo the flashing as part of a <a href="/roof-replacement-process/">full replacement</a>. Ask whether your quote includes new flashing rather than reusing old pieces. See <a href="/roof-repair-or-replace/">repair or replace</a>.</p>
<h2>Questions for your roofer</h2><ul><li>Will you install new step and counter flashing, or reuse the old?</li><li>Is drip edge included? Many codes now require it.</li><li>What material are the pipe boots, and how long do they last?</li></ul>""",
 "Roof flashing", "crew1",
 [("What is the most common cause of roof leaks?", "Failing flashing and cracked pipe boots are among the most common. Leaks tend to start where the roof meets something else."),
  ("Can flashing be repaired without a new roof?", "Often yes. A roofer can replace flashing or a pipe boot and weave in matching shingles if the rest of the roof is sound."),
  ("Should old flashing be reused on a new roof?", "Many roofers replace it, especially step flashing and drip edge, because old flashing can be bent or corroded. Ask what your quote includes."),
  ("Is caulk enough to fix flashing?", "Caulk or roofing cement is a short-term patch. A lasting fix is properly installed flashing.")],
 "Types of flashing, warning signs, and repair vs replace.",
 ["/roof-leak/", "/roof-repair-or-replace/", "/roof-inspection/", "/roofing-estimate/"]),

("/roof-underlayment/", "Roof Underlayment: Felt vs Synthetic | Bedrock Roof", "Roof underlayment: felt vs. synthetic, and why it matters",
 "What roof underlayment does, felt vs synthetic vs ice-and-water shield compared, where each is used, and what to look for in a roofing quote.",
 "Underlayment is the layer between the roof deck and the shingles or metal. It's your backup when water gets past the top layer.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Underlayment is a water-resistant layer installed over the roof deck before shingles, metal or tile go on. Most new residential roofs today use synthetic underlayment, which is lighter, stronger and less prone to tearing than traditional asphalt felt. Self-adhering ice-and-water membrane is added at eaves, valleys and around openings, especially where ice dams or wind-driven rain are a risk.</div>
<h2>The main types</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>Where it's used</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Asphalt felt (#15 or #30)</td><td>Paper or fiberglass mat soaked in asphalt</td><td>Traditional; lower cost; can wrinkle when wet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Synthetic</td><td>Woven or spun polymer sheets</td><td>Most common on new roofs; lighter and tear-resistant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-adhering membrane (ice and water shield)</td><td>Rubberized asphalt with a sticky back that seals around nails</td><td>Eaves, valleys, around chimneys and skylights; whole-roof in some high-wind and coastal areas</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Why it matters</h2><ul><li>It keeps the deck dry during installation.</li><li>It's a second barrier if shingles blow off or water is driven under them.</li><li>Some shingle warranties require specific underlayment products.</li><li>Codes in cold climates often require ice barrier at the eaves, and some coastal areas encourage or require a sealed roof deck.</li></ul>
<h2>What to look for in a quote</h2><ul><li>The underlayment type and brand, not just "underlayment".</li><li>Where ice-and-water membrane will go (eaves, valleys, around openings, or the whole roof).</li><li>Whether it meets the shingle maker's requirements for the warranty you're being sold.</li></ul>
<p>Compare quotes line by line with our <a href="/roofing-estimate/">guide to reading a roofing estimate</a>.</p>""",
 "Underlayment", "tearoff",
 [("Is synthetic underlayment better than felt?", "For most new roofs, yes. It's lighter, stronger and resists tearing and wrinkling. Felt is cheaper and still meets code in many places."),
  ("What is ice and water shield?", "A self-adhering rubberized membrane that seals around nails. It goes at eaves, valleys and openings where water is most likely to back up."),
  ("Do I need ice and water shield on the whole roof?", "Not usually. It's commonly required at the eaves in cold climates. In some high-wind or coastal areas, a fully sealed deck is recommended."),
  ("Can underlayment be replaced without new shingles?", "No. It sits under the shingles, so replacing it means removing the roof covering.")],
 "Felt vs synthetic vs ice-and-water shield.",
 ["/roofing-estimate/", "/ice-dams/", "/roof-replacement-process/", "/roofing-materials/"]),

("/tile-roof/", "Tile Roofs: Clay vs Concrete, Lifespan and Repairs | Bedrock Roof", "Tile roofs: clay vs. concrete, lifespan and repairs",
 "Clay and concrete tile roofs explained: how long they last, weight, cost compared with shingles, why the underlayment matters, and common repairs.",
 "Tile roofs are common in Florida, the Southwest and California. Here's what owners and buyers should know.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Clay and concrete tiles can last 50 years or more, and they resist fire and heat well. They cost more than shingles and are much heavier, so the framing must be able to carry them. On many tile roofs, the underlayment beneath the tiles wears out long before the tiles do, so a "tile roof replacement" sometimes means lifting the tiles, replacing the underlayment and putting the tiles back.</div>
<h2>Clay vs. concrete</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Clay tile</th><th>Concrete tile</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Look</td><td>Classic terracotta; color goes through the tile</td><td>Many shapes and colors; some colors fade over time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight</td><td>Heavy</td><td>Usually heavier than clay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Generally higher</td><td>Generally lower than clay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lifespan</td><td>Often 50+ years</td><td>Often 40&ndash;50+ years</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Strengths and weak spots</h2><ul><li><b>Strengths:</b> long life, Class A fire ratings, good in heat, and a look many buyers want.</li><li><b>Weak spots:</b> tiles can crack if walked on or hit by large hail, and the underlayment underneath ages.</li></ul>
<h2>Common repairs</h2><ul><li>Replacing cracked or slipped tiles. Keep spare tiles from the original job if you can, since matching older tiles can be hard.</li><li>Re-setting ridge and hip tiles.</li><li>Fixing flashing at valleys and walls. See <a href="/roof-flashing/">roof flashing</a>.</li><li>Lift-and-relay: removing tiles, installing new underlayment and reinstalling the tiles.</li></ul>
<h2>Hiring for tile work</h2><p>Not every roofer does tile. Ask how many tile roofs the crew has done, how they walk on the roof without cracking tiles, and whether the quote includes new underlayment and flashing. In hurricane areas, ask how the tiles will be fastened to meet local wind rules.</p>""",
 "Tile roofs", "home5",
 [("How long does a tile roof last?", "The tiles often last 50 years or more. The underlayment beneath them usually needs replacing sooner, which can be done by lifting and relaying the tiles."),
  ("Can you walk on a tile roof?", "Carefully, and ideally only by an experienced roofer. Tiles can crack under weight if stepped on in the wrong place."),
  ("Is tile more expensive than shingles?", "Yes, tile generally costs more to buy and install, but it can last much longer."),
  ("Can my house support a tile roof?", "Tile is heavy. Switching from shingles to tile may need an engineer to confirm the framing can carry it.")],
 "Clay vs concrete, lifespan, underlayment and repairs.",
 ["/roofing-materials/", "/how-long-does-a-roof-last/", "/roof-underlayment/", "/roof-flashing/"]),
("/tpo-vs-epdm/", "TPO vs EPDM vs PVC: Flat Roof Membranes Compared | Bedrock Roof", "TPO vs. EPDM vs. PVC: which flat roof membrane is best?",
 "TPO, EPDM and PVC single-ply roofing compared for commercial and low-slope roofs: seams, reflectivity, chemical resistance, climate fit and repairs.",
 "The three most common single-ply membranes on U.S. commercial roofs, side by side.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> TPO is the most common choice on new low-slope commercial roofs. It's usually white and reflective, with heat-welded seams. EPDM is a rubber membrane with the longest track record, often used in colder climates and on buildings where cooling cost matters less. PVC costs more but resists grease and chemicals, so it's a common pick for restaurants and some industrial roofs. Installation quality, especially at seams, edges and penetrations, matters as much as which membrane you choose.</div>
<h2>Side by side</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>TPO</th><th>EPDM</th><th>PVC</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Material</td><td>Thermoplastic polyolefin</td><td>Synthetic rubber</td><td>Polyvinyl chloride</td></tr>
<tr><td>Usual color</td><td>White (reflective)</td><td>Black (white available)</td><td>White (reflective)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seams</td><td>Heat-welded</td><td>Taped or glued</td><td>Heat-welded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grease and chemicals</td><td>Fair</td><td>Poor with oils and grease</td><td>Good</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical fit</td><td>Warm and mixed climates; most new commercial roofs</td><td>Cold climates; budget-conscious owners</td><td>Restaurants, kitchens, some industrial uses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relative cost</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Often lowest</td><td>Highest of the three</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>How to choose</h2><ul><li><b>Climate and energy:</b> a white membrane reflects sunlight and can lower cooling loads in hot regions. Some local energy codes require a reflective ("cool") roof on low-slope commercial buildings.</li><li><b>Roof traffic:</b> if HVAC techs are on the roof often, ask about thicker membranes and walkway pads.</li><li><b>Exhaust and grease:</b> kitchen exhaust can damage EPDM and TPO; PVC or a protective pad near fans is common.</li><li><b>Attachment:</b> membranes can be mechanically fastened, fully adhered or ballasted. Wind exposure, deck type and budget decide which.</li><li><b>Insulation:</b> a replacement is often the time to add insulation under the membrane to meet current energy code.</li></ul>
<h2>Questions for bidders</h2><ul><li>What membrane thickness (mils) and attachment method are you quoting?</li><li>Is this a manufacturer-warranted system, and for how many years?</li><li>Will you remove the existing roof or recover over it, and did you take core samples to check for wet insulation?</li></ul>
<p>More: <a href="/flat-roof-types/">flat roof types</a>, <a href="/commercial-roof-coatings/">roof coatings</a> and <a href="/commercial-roofing/">commercial roofing</a>.</p>""",
 "TPO vs EPDM vs PVC", "roofsky",
 [("Is TPO better than EPDM?", "Neither is best everywhere. TPO is reflective with welded seams and suits hot climates. EPDM has a long track record and suits colder climates and tighter budgets."),
  ("Which flat roof membrane lasts longest?", "Lifespans overlap and depend heavily on installation, thickness and maintenance. Ask each bidder what warranty term the manufacturer will issue for the exact system quoted."),
  ("Why do restaurants use PVC roofs?", "PVC resists grease and many chemicals better than TPO or EPDM, which matters near kitchen exhaust fans."),
  ("Can a new membrane go over the old roof?", "Sometimes. Codes usually limit the number of roof layers, and wet insulation must be removed first. Core samples or a moisture survey tell you what's under there.")],
 "Single-ply membranes compared for commercial roofs.",
 ["/flat-roof-types/", "/commercial-roofing/", "/commercial-roof-coatings/", "/commercial-roof-replacement-cost/"]),

("/commercial-roof-replacement-cost/", "Commercial Roof Replacement Cost: What Drives the Price | Bedrock Roof", "What drives commercial roof replacement cost?",
 "What affects the cost of replacing a commercial or flat roof: membrane type, tear-off vs recover, insulation, roof size and access, permits, and warranty level.",
 "Commercial roof prices vary widely, so it helps to know what's actually driving the number in each bid.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Commercial roofs are usually priced per square foot or per "square" (100 square feet), and the biggest drivers are the roof system, whether the old roof must be torn off, how much insulation is added, the building's height and access, and the warranty you want. Bids for the same building can differ a lot, so compare scope line by line rather than just the total. We don't publish a single average price, because it would be misleading across U.S. regions and building types.</div>
<h2>The main cost drivers</h2><ul><li><b>Roof system:</b> TPO, EPDM, PVC, modified bitumen, built-up, metal or a coating. See <a href="/tpo-vs-epdm/">TPO vs EPDM vs PVC</a>.</li><li><b>Tear-off or recover:</b> removing old layers costs more but finds wet insulation and deck damage. Codes limit how many layers you can have.</li><li><b>Insulation:</b> energy codes often require added insulation on a replacement, and tapered insulation fixes ponding water.</li><li><b>Deck repairs:</b> rusted metal deck or rotted wood is often priced per unit once exposed. Ask for those unit prices in the bid.</li><li><b>Penetrations and details:</b> HVAC curbs, drains, skylights, parapet walls and edge metal add labor.</li><li><b>Access and height:</b> cranes, hoists, street permits and working around tenants or business hours.</li><li><b>Warranty level:</b> a manufacturer's full system warranty usually requires specific materials and inspections, which can raise cost.</li></ul>
<h2>How to compare bids</h2><ol><li>Give every bidder the same scope, ideally in writing.</li><li>Ask for core samples or an infrared or moisture survey so everyone prices the same conditions.</li><li>Get unit prices for deck and insulation replacement.</li><li>Confirm who pulls the permit and who handles HVAC disconnects.</li><li>Check the contractor's license and insurance with your state. See <a href="/choosing-a-roofer/">choosing a roofer</a>.</li></ol>
<h2>Repair, coat or replace?</h2><p>If the insulation is mostly dry and the membrane is sound, a repair or a <a href="/commercial-roof-coatings/">restoration coating</a> may extend the roof's life for less. Widespread wet insulation or repeated leaks usually mean replacement. Planned maintenance helps either way; see the <a href="/commercial-roof-maintenance/">maintenance checklist</a>.</p>""",
 "Commercial roof cost", "crew1",
 [("How is commercial roofing priced?", "Usually per square foot or per square (100 square feet), plus line items for tear-off, insulation, deck repairs, flashing and edge metal."),
  ("Why are commercial roof bids so different?", "Bidders often assume different scopes, such as recover vs tear-off, insulation thickness or warranty level. Give everyone the same written scope."),
  ("Is a roof coating cheaper than replacement?", "Usually, when the existing roof is sound and mostly dry. A coating won't fix wet insulation or a failing deck."),
  ("Can roof replacement be deducted for a business?", "Tax treatment of building improvements varies. Ask your accountant how a roof replacement is handled for your property.")],
 "Membrane, tear-off, insulation, access and warranty.",
 ["/commercial-roofing/", "/tpo-vs-epdm/", "/commercial-roof-coatings/", "/commercial-roof-maintenance/"]),

("/commercial-roof-leak/", "Commercial Roof Leak: What to Do First | Bedrock Roof", "Commercial or flat roof leak: what to do first",
 "Steps for property managers when a commercial or flat roof leaks: protect people and equipment, document, find the source, temporary repairs and warranty claims.",
 "Leaks on flat roofs are hard to trace because water travels. Here's how to respond quickly and in the right order.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Protect people and equipment first, then document the leak with photos, times and weather. Check for clogged drains and ponding water, which cause many flat-roof leaks. If the roof is under a manufacturer or contractor warranty, call the warranty holder before anyone else works on it, so the warranty isn't voided. Water travels under membranes, so the leak inside is often not directly under the hole.</div>
<h2>First hour</h2><ol><li>Move people and equipment, and cut power to anything wet if it's safe to do so.</li><li>Contain water with buckets, tarps and plastic sheeting.</li><li>Photograph the ceiling, floor, and any staining; note the time and the weather.</li><li>If it's safe, have maintenance staff check that roof drains and scuppers aren't clogged.</li></ol>
<h2>Finding the source</h2><ul><li>Common culprits: clogged drains, seams, flashing at walls and curbs, HVAC units, pitch pans and roof edges.</li><li>Ponding water that sits more than a couple of days after rain stresses membranes and seams.</li><li>Infrared or electronic leak detection can locate wet insulation and breaches without tearing up the roof.</li></ul>
<h2>Warranty and insurance</h2><ul><li>Check whether the roof has a manufacturer warranty and a separate contractor workmanship warranty. Many require that only authorized contractors make repairs.</li><li>Storm-related leaks may be covered by property insurance. Document everything and notify your carrier promptly. See <a href="/roof-insurance-claim/">roof insurance claims</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>Preventing the next one</h2><p>Twice-yearly inspections and after-storm checks catch most problems early. Use our <a href="/commercial-roof-maintenance/">commercial roof maintenance checklist</a>.</p>""",
 "Commercial roof leak", "crew3",
 [("Why does my flat roof leak only sometimes?", "Leaks that appear with wind-driven rain or heavy storms often come from flashing, edges or HVAC curbs. Leaks after any rain often point to drains, seams or punctures."),
  ("Is the leak above where the water drips?", "Not always. Water can travel across the deck or through insulation before it shows up inside."),
  ("Should I call the original installer?", "If the roof is under warranty, yes. Repairs by others can void some warranties."),
  ("How fast should a commercial leak be fixed?", "As soon as possible. Wet insulation loses value and can lead to deck rust or rot and mold.")],
 "Protect, document, trace the source and use the warranty.",
 ["/commercial-roof-maintenance/", "/commercial-roofing/", "/roof-leak/", "/commercial-roof-warranty/"]),

("/commercial-roof-warranty/", "Commercial Roof Warranties Explained | Bedrock Roof", "Commercial roof warranties: manufacturer vs. contractor",
 "How commercial roof warranties work: manufacturer NDL system warranties, material-only warranties, contractor workmanship warranties, and what voids them.",
 "A commercial roof warranty is only as useful as its fine print. Here's what the common types cover.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Commercial roofs usually carry two kinds of warranty. A manufacturer warranty covers materials and, in the stronger "system" or "no dollar limit" (NDL) versions, the cost of repairing leaks caused by materials or installation, for a set number of years. A contractor workmanship warranty covers installation errors, often for a shorter period. Read both, since exclusions and maintenance requirements decide whether they pay.</div>
<h2>Common warranty types</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Usually covers</th><th>Watch for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Material-only</td><td>Defective membrane or components</td><td>May not cover labor to fix leaks</td></tr>
<tr><td>System / NDL</td><td>Leak repairs from materials or workmanship, no dollar cap</td><td>Requires approved contractor, specific materials and a final inspection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contractor workmanship</td><td>Installation mistakes</td><td>Often shorter; only as good as the contractor's staying power</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>What commonly voids or limits coverage</h2><ul><li>Repairs or new penetrations by unauthorized contractors.</li><li>Skipped maintenance, like clogged drains or debris left on the roof.</li><li>Damage from foot traffic, other trades or rooftop equipment.</li><li>Severe weather beyond stated wind speeds; check the wind rating.</li><li>Not registering the warranty or paying the issuing fee.</li></ul>
<h2>Tips before you sign</h2><ul><li>Ask for a sample warranty document from the manufacturer, not a brochure.</li><li>Confirm the term, wind speed rating and whether it transfers if you sell.</li><li>Keep inspection and maintenance records on file.</li></ul>
<p>Homeowner version: <a href="/roof-warranty/">residential roof warranties</a>.</p>""",
 "Commercial warranty", "roofsky",
 [("What is an NDL roof warranty?", "No Dollar Limit: a manufacturer system warranty that pays for covered leak repairs without a cap on the dollar amount, for its term."),
  ("Who issues a commercial roof warranty?", "The membrane manufacturer issues the material or system warranty; the installing contractor issues the workmanship warranty."),
  ("Does maintenance affect my roof warranty?", "Often yes. Many warranties require reasonable maintenance and may exclude damage from clogged drains or debris."),
  ("Can I transfer a commercial roof warranty?", "Some are transferable with notice and a fee. Check the terms before you sell.")],
 "Manufacturer vs contractor, NDL and what voids coverage.",
 ["/commercial-roofing/", "/commercial-roof-maintenance/", "/roof-warranty/", "/tpo-vs-epdm/"]),

("/hoa-roof-replacement/", "HOA and Condo Roof Replacement: A Board's Guide | Bedrock Roof", "HOA and condo roof replacement: a guide for boards",
 "How HOA and condo boards can plan a roof replacement: reserve studies, who is responsible, bidding, communicating with owners, insurance and scheduling.",
 "Replacing roofs across a community is a big project. Here's how boards and managers keep it organized and fair.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>Quick answer:</b> Start with your governing documents to see whether the association or individual owners are responsible for roofs. Use a reserve study and a professional roof assessment to plan timing and budget. Then bid the work with one written scope, check licenses and insurance, and communicate the schedule clearly to owners. Many associations phase replacements over several years to match reserves.</div>
<h2>1. Know who is responsible</h2><p>CC&amp;Rs and bylaws decide whether the association or each owner maintains and replaces roofs, and state law can affect this, especially for condominiums. If it's unclear, ask the association's attorney before bidding.</p>
<h2>2. Plan with data</h2><ul><li>A <b>reserve study</b> estimates when roofs will need replacing and how much to set aside.</li><li>A <b>roof condition assessment</b> by a qualified roofer or consultant ranks buildings by need, with photos.</li><li>Phasing the worst roofs first can spread cost and avoid special assessments.</li></ul>
<h2>3. Bid it right</h2><ul><li>Write one scope: materials, colors approved by the architectural committee, underlayment, flashing, ventilation, cleanup and warranty.</li><li>Get at least three bids from licensed, insured roofers. Ask for references from other communities.</li><li>Confirm who pulls permits and how damage to landscaping, cars and gutters is handled.</li></ul>
<h2>4. Communicate with owners</h2><p>Send schedules and notices well ahead: which buildings, which days, parking changes, noise hours, and who to call with concerns. Ask owners to move vehicles and fragile items from walls during tear-off.</p>
<h2>5. Insurance and storms</h2><p>After hail or wind, check the master policy and deductibles before signing anything. Be wary of contractors going door-to-door asking individual owners to sign. See <a href="/roofing-scams/">roofing scams</a> and <a href="/roof-insurance-claim/">insurance claims</a>.</p>""",
 "HOA roof replacement", "home4",
 [("Who pays for roof replacement in an HOA?", "It depends on the governing documents and state law. In many condominiums the association pays from reserves; in many single-family HOAs owners pay."),
  ("Does an HOA need multiple roofing bids?", "Many boards get at least three, and some governing documents or state laws require competitive bidding for large projects."),
  ("Can an HOA require a specific roof color or material?", "Usually, through architectural guidelines, though some states limit restrictions on certain materials like solar or impact-resistant shingles."),
  ("How long does a community roof project take?", "Each building often takes a few days, weather permitting. A whole community may be phased over weeks or years.")],
 "Responsibility, reserve studies, bidding and owner notices.",
 ["/commercial-roofing/", "/roofing-estimate/", "/insurance-roof-replacement/", "/choosing-a-roofer/"]),
("/free-roof-inspection/", "Free Roof Inspection: Get One From a Local Roofer | Bedrock Roof", "Get a free roof inspection from a local roofer",
 "Request a free, no-obligation roof inspection from an independent local roofer anywhere in the U.S. What's checked, how it works, and what you get afterward.",
 "Tell us about your roof and we'll connect you with an independent local roofer for a free inspection and a written quote.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>How it works:</b> fill in the short form on this page (about 30 seconds). We match your request with an independent roofer who serves your ZIP code. They call or text you to set up a time, inspect the roof, and give you their findings and a written quote if work is needed. The inspection is free and you don't have to hire anyone.</div>
<h2>Good times to get a free inspection</h2><ul><li>After hail, high wind or a hurricane, even if you don't see damage from the ground.</li><li>When you find a leak, a ceiling stain or shingles in the yard.</li><li>When the roof is 15 years old or more.</li><li>Before you buy or sell a home.</li><li>If your insurer has asked about your roof's age or condition.</li></ul>
<h2>What the roofer checks</h2><p>Shingles or panels, flashing around chimneys and walls, pipe boots and vents, gutters, signs of hail or wind damage, and the attic if it's accessible. Ask for photos of anything they find. See <a href="/roof-inspection/">what happens during a roof inspection</a>.</p>
<h2>Why use Bedrock Roof</h2><ul><li><b>Free for you.</b> You never pay us. Roofers may pay us for introductions.</li><li><b>One local roofer, not a flood of calls.</b> We try to match each request with one roofer who serves your area.</li><li><b>No pressure.</b> You can get more quotes and take your time. Our <a href="/choosing-a-roofer/">roofer checklist</a> helps you compare.</li></ul>
<p class="small">Bedrock Roof is a referral service, not a roofing contractor. Roofers are independent businesses. Check any contractor's license and insurance before you sign.</p>""",
 "Free roof inspection", "crew2",
 [("Is the roof inspection really free?", "Yes. The roofer we connect you with offers a free inspection and quote, and you never pay Bedrock Roof. You only pay if you choose to hire a roofer for work."),
  ("How soon can someone come out?", "It depends on the roofer's schedule and the weather, especially after big storms. Most roofers reach out within a business day to set a time."),
  ("Will the roofer help with my insurance claim?", "Many roofers document storm damage with photos and an estimate you can give your insurer. Your insurance company decides what's covered."),
  ("Do you inspect commercial roofs?", "Yes. Choose Commercial or Apts / HOA on the form and we'll look for a commercial roofer. See our <a href=\"/commercial-roof-inspection/\">commercial inspection page</a>.")],
 "Request a free inspection from a local roofer.",
 ["/roof-inspection/", "/storm-damage/", "/signs-you-need-a-new-roof/", "/choosing-a-roofer/"]),

("/commercial-roof-inspection/", "Free Commercial Roof Inspection and Quote | Bedrock Roof", "Free commercial roof inspection and quote",
 "Owners and property managers: request a free commercial or flat roof inspection and quote from an independent commercial roofer. What's checked and what to ask for.",
 "For offices, retail, warehouses, churches, apartments, condos and HOAs. We connect you with an independent commercial roofer for a free inspection.",
 """<div class="callout quick"><b>How it works:</b> send the short form on this page with Commercial or Apts / HOA selected. We look for an independent commercial roofer serving your ZIP code, who contacts you to inspect the roof and give you a written report and quote. It's free and there's no obligation.</div>
<h2>What a commercial inspection usually covers</h2><ul><li>Membrane condition, seams and punctures on TPO, EPDM, PVC, modified bitumen or built-up roofs.</li><li>Ponding water, drains, scuppers and gutters.</li><li>Flashing at parapet walls, curbs, HVAC units and other penetrations.</li><li>Roof edges and metal coping.</li><li>Signs of wet insulation; ask whether core samples or an infrared scan are recommended.</li></ul>
<h2>When to request one</h2><ul><li>Twice a year as routine maintenance, ideally spring and fall.</li><li>After hail, high wind or heavy snow.</li><li>When tenants report leaks or stains.</li><li>Before buying a building, renewing insurance or planning a reserve study.</li></ul>
<h2>What to ask for afterward</h2><p>Photos, a written condition report, and options: repair, restoration coating, or replacement, each with scope and warranty. Our guides on <a href="/commercial-roof-replacement-cost/">commercial roof cost drivers</a>, <a href="/tpo-vs-epdm/">TPO vs EPDM vs PVC</a> and <a href="/commercial-roof-warranty/">commercial warranties</a> help you compare.</p>
<p class="small">Bedrock Roof is a referral service, not a roofing contractor. Verify any contractor's license and insurance before signing.</p>""",
 "Commercial inspection", "roofsky",
 [("Is a commercial roof inspection free?", "The commercial roofer we connect you with offers a free inspection and quote. Specialized testing, like an infrared moisture survey, may cost extra; ask first."),
  ("How often should a commercial roof be inspected?", "Most roofing groups recommend twice a year and after major storms."),
  ("Can you help HOAs and apartment owners?", "Yes. Pick Apts / HOA on the form. See our <a href=\"/hoa-roof-replacement/\">HOA roof replacement guide</a>."),
  ("Do I need to be the owner?", "Owners, property managers and board members can all request an inspection.")],
 "Free inspection for commercial, apartment and HOA roofs.",
 ["/commercial-roofing/", "/commercial-roof-maintenance/", "/commercial-roof-leak/", "/tpo-vs-epdm/"]),
]
